  "worked_solution": "The points of inflection are the points where \n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph. \n\n- The point $x=-2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$ Second, the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=3$ is not a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=3,$  the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
